# Lessons — what the working taught, in this project

Append-only. One entry per lesson, written **in the turn the lesson appears**, never at the
end of a session and never from memory. Ids are sequential from `L-0001`; the stamp is
`date -u +'%Y-%m-%d %H:%M UTC'` at the moment of writing.

**This file starts empty, and that is the point.** The practices shipped with the bundle
(`skills/verifiable-gates/references/working.md`) came from somebody else's ledger and cost
somebody else twenty-one rounds. Yours will be better for your project than theirs, and the
first entry is usually a guard that did not guard.

Never edit or delete an entry. If a later lesson overturns an earlier one, write a new
entry and put `updates: L-00NN` in its header — the history of having been wrong is the
part that carries. Before adding one, read the ledger: if an entry already covers it, say
so in `updates:` rather than writing a second.

Keep this file out of the tree unless you mean to publish it: add `.local/` to
`.gitignore`, or to `.git/info/exclude` if the decision is yours alone rather than the
team's.

## The shape

```
## L-00NN — <title: the lesson in one line>
stamp: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM UTC · updates: L-00xx (or —)
**Context:** what happened, with the concrete evidence — the command, the run id, the
number measured. Facts, not impressions.
**Lesson:** the rule in one or two sentences.
**Apply:** the exact guard, command or habit that follows from it.
```

`Apply` is what makes an entry worth keeping. A lesson with no `Apply` is a story; one with
an `Apply` somebody can follow is a practice waiting for its third pull request.

